  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 248 PM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nFriday through Wednesday:\n\nSurface high pressure from the Northern Plains will finally \npush into Wisconsin by Friday, in the wake of an upper trough \nthat swings across Lake Superior. Northerly winds up to 20 MPH \nin the morning will diminish to 15 MPH by late afternoon. Expect\na sunny day with highs in the lower to mid 60s, which is \nslightly below normal. Lows Fri night will dip down to around 40\nfor most of southern Wisconsin, but remain in the mid/upper 40s\nright along the Lake Michigan shoreline. \n\nFor Saturday, a weak trough will approach Wisconsin from the \nDakotas. This will give us a wind switch to the south with the \ndeparting high, and some increasing middle and high clouds, \nespecially toward evening. Highs Saturday will be in the middle \n60s, although cooler by Lake Michigan due to a southeast \ncomponent to the wind. Light rain showers associated with the \npassage of a surface and upper level trough should remain north \nof southern Wisconsin Saturday afternoon and night, due to low \nlevel dry air, although cannot rule out a few sprinkles. Lows \nSaturday night will be in the upper 40s.\n\nExpect sunshine and a northwest breeze Sunday, with highs in \nthe upper 60s. We will have a couple of cooler nights Sunday and\nMonday night, with high pressure drifting through the Upper \nGreat Lakes region. With dewpoints expected to be close to the \nlow temperature values in the upper 30s to around 40 each \nnight, areas of fog may develop.\n\nWe will have a warming trend Tuesday and Wednesday, with \nWednesday highs around 70. Another upper trough is expected to \ncross Ontario Wednesday. The latest run of the GFS has a more \namplified trough than the ECMWF, and ensembles support this \ndifference as well. If the trough is more amplified, the chance \nof rain showers will increase for southern Wisconsin.\n\nCronce",
